{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775333448-CDMjcuIDrv1WPEnNIUr4","role":"assistant","created":1775333461,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The value -1.103 looks reasonable. Let me check my data extraction again to make sure I'm using the correct paper money values. I'm looking at the \"Total paper money\" column from FY 1941 to FY 1950.\n\nLooking at my data:\n- FY 1941: 8,932 (from 1948 bulletin, column \"Total paper money\")\n- FY 1942: 11,600\n- FY 1943: 16,492 (but 1950 bulletin shows 16,492, consistent)\n- FY 1944: 21,438 (but 1950 bulletin shows 21,438)\n- FY 1945: 25,511 (but 1950 bulletin shows 25,511, vs 25,541 in 1948 bulletin - there seems to be a revision)\n\nWait, there's a discrepancy in the 1945 value:\n- 1948 bulletin: 25,541\n- 1950 bulletin: 25,511\n\nI should check which one is correct. The 1950 bulletin is more recent, so I should use the values from that. Let me verify the data extraction again:\n\nFrom the 1950 bulletin page 56 (the final, most updated source):\n- 1943: 16,492\n- 1944: 21,438\n- 1945: 25,511\n- 1946: 26,945\n- 1947: 26,942\n- 1948: 26,182\n- 1949: 26,034\n- 1950: 25,661\n\nAnd from the 1948 bulletin (for 1941-1942):\n- 1941: 8,932\n- 1942: 11,600\n\nThe data looks correct. Now I need to confirm my formula is correct.\n\nThe unbiased sample Fisher excess kurtosis for the data is approximately -1.103.\n\n\nI'll verify the calculation by comparing with R's formulas. There seem to be multiple approaches to computing kurtosis, so I'll cross-reference to ensure accuracy. I'll specifically check the SAS definition to validate my current method and confirm the result matches my previous calculation.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_078cd0f579c0469dbbe2cab1","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"python3 << 'EOF'\n# Let me also verify with SAS-style formula\n# SAS uses: G2 = ((n-1)/((n-2)(n-3))) * ((n+1)*g2 + 6)\n# where g2 is computed with biased estimator using ddof=1\n\ndata = [8932, 11600, 16492, 21438, 25511, 26945, 26942, 26182, 26034, 25661]\nn = len(data)\nmean = sum(data) / n\nstd = math.sqrt(sum((x - mean) ** 2 for x in data) / (n - 1))\n\n# g2 (biased) using sample std\nmoment4 = sum((x - mean) ** 4 for x in data) / n\ng2 = moment4 / (std ** 4) - 3\n\n# SAS formula \nG2_SAS = ((n - 1) / ((n - 2) * (n - 3))) * ((n + 1) * g2 + 6)\n\nprint(f\"Data: {data}\")\nprint(f\"g2 (biased): {g2}\")\nprint(f\"SAS formula G2: {G2_SAS}\")\n\n# Also compute the Excel/traditional formula \n# (which uses n-1 bias correction differently)\n# Excel's KURT: =((n*(n+1))/((n-1)*(n-2)*(n-3))*sum((x-mean)^4)/s^4) - (3*(n-1)^2)/((n-2)*(n-3))\n\ns4 = std ** 4\nsum_4th = sum((x - mean) ** 4 for x in data)\n\nExcel_G2 = (n * (n + 1) / ((n - 1) * (n - 2) * (n - 3))) * (sum_4th / s4) - (3 * (n - 1) ** 2) / ((n - 2) * (n - 3))\n\nprint(f\"Excel formula G2: {Excel_G2}\")\n\n# The standard answer should be consistent with scipy.stats.kurtosis(fisher=True, bias=False)\n# which is: -1.103\n\nprint(f\"\\nFinal answer: {round(G2_SAS, 3)}\")\nEOF"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
